WEBVTT
TITLE Probability, Random Variables and Distributions — GATE Visual Explainer
NOTE Timings are estimated from the lesson timeline (speaking-rate based), not measured from recorded audio.

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.500
Probability starts with a set of outcomes and a rule that gives every event a number.

2
00:00:07.500 --> 00:00:15.000
P(sum = 7) = 6/36 = 1/6, because six of the thirty-six outcomes give seven.

3
00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:22.500
P(sum > 9) is also 6/36: the sums 10, 11 and 12 carry three, two and one outcomes.

4
00:00:22.500 --> 00:00:30.000
The three axioms: P(S) = 1, every probability lies between 0 and 1, and disjoint events add.

5
00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:37.500
Conditioning on B throws away every outcome outside B and renormalises what is left.

6
00:00:37.500 --> 00:00:45.000
The event "sum > 9" keeps 6 of the 36 outcomes, so the new sample space has size 6.

7
00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:52.500
Inside it, 2 outcomes give 11, so P(sum = 11 | sum > 9) = 2/6 = 1/3.

8
00:00:52.500 --> 00:01:00.000
Independence is P(A and B) = P(A)P(B); mutual exclusivity is P(A and B) = 0. They are not the same.

9
00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:07.500
Bayes' theorem turns P(evidence | cause) into P(cause | evidence).

10
00:01:07.500 --> 00:01:15.000
GATE 2021 Set 1 Q54: H is transmitted with probability 0.1, L with 0.9.

11
00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:22.500
P(H received) = 0.3(0.1) + 0.8(0.9) = 0.75, by the law of total probability.

12
00:01:22.500 --> 00:01:30.000
P(H sent | H received) = 0.03 / 0.75 = 0.04. The posterior sits below the prior.

13
00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:37.500
A random variable is a function: it assigns a number to each outcome.

14
00:01:37.500 --> 00:01:45.000
For a discrete variable the PMF gives each value its probability, and those probabilities sum to 1.

15
00:01:45.000 --> 00:01:52.500
The CDF F(x) = P(X <= x) is a staircase: non-decreasing, flat between values, reaching 1.

16
00:01:52.500 --> 00:02:00.000
For a continuous variable only intervals carry probability: P(X = a) = 0 for every a.

17
00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:07.500
The expectation is the probability-weighted average of the values: the balance point of the PMF.

18
00:02:07.500 --> 00:02:15.000
GATE 2026 Set 1: eight values carry weights of one sixth and one twelfth.

19
00:02:15.000 --> 00:02:22.500
E[X] = 17/4 = 4.25, a weighted sum and not a plain average of one to eight.

20
00:02:22.500 --> 00:02:30.000
Linearity always holds: E[X + Y] = E[X] + E[Y]. E[XY] = E[X]E[Y] needs independence.

21
00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:37.500
Two distributions can share a mean and differ completely in spread.

22
00:02:37.500 --> 00:02:45.000
Variance is the mean squared distance from the mean: 5.5208 here, so the standard deviation is 2.3496.

23
00:02:45.000 --> 00:02:52.500
Var(aX + b) = a squared times Var(X): shifting does not change spread, scaling squares it.

24
00:02:52.500 --> 00:03:00.000
Mean, median and mode coincide only for symmetric data; the median of max(X, 0) is 0.

25
00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:07.500
A Bernoulli trial has two outcomes; the binomial counts successes in n independent trials.

26
00:03:07.500 --> 00:03:15.000
The binomial PMF is C(n, k) p^k (1-p)^(n-k), and its values sum to 1.

27
00:03:15.000 --> 00:03:22.500
Mean np = 3 and variance np(1-p) = 2.1 for n = 10 and p = 0.3.

28
00:03:22.500 --> 00:03:30.000
P(X = 3) = 0.2668: here the mode and the mean coincide at 3.

29
00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:37.500
The Poisson counts events in a fixed interval when they occur independently at a constant rate.

30
00:03:37.500 --> 00:03:45.000
Its PMF is e to the minus lambda, lambda to the k, over k factorial, with mean and variance both lambda.

31
00:03:45.000 --> 00:03:52.500
With lambda = 3 the probability of no event is 0.0498, and of exactly three events 0.2240.

32
00:03:52.500 --> 00:04:00.000
Poisson is the limit of binomial when n is large and p is small with np = lambda: the gap here is 0.0015.

33
00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:07.500
The exponential is the continuous waiting time between Poisson events.

34
00:04:07.500 --> 00:04:15.000
Its density is lambda e to the minus lambda x, so the area beyond 4 is e to the minus 1, which is 0.3679.

35
00:04:15.000 --> 00:04:22.500
It is memoryless: P(X > 7 | X > 3) = P(X > 4) = 0.3679.

36
00:04:22.500 --> 00:04:30.000
Mean 1/lambda = 4, variance 1/lambda squared = 16, and the median is below the mean.

37
00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:37.500
The normal is fixed by its mean and variance: 68.27, 95.45 and 99.73 percent within one, two and three sigma.

38
00:04:37.500 --> 00:04:45.000
Standardise with z = (x - mu)/sigma to turn any normal into the standard one.

39
00:04:45.000 --> 00:04:52.500
GATE 2026 Set 2: the density one over three root two pi, e to the minus x squared over eighteen, is normal with mean 0 and variance 9.

40
00:04:52.500 --> 00:05:00.000
Procedure: name the variable type, write the question as an event, then compute — and check the answer is a probability.

